  title: "Yield — The Number That Determines Whether a Great Chip Process Is Economically Great",
  date: "2026-08-15",
  body: `Prior Lessons — 1-Sentence Recap

Lesson 004: Chips begin on ultra-pure 300mm silicon wafers, and wafer quality matters because tiny starting defects can destroy value after expensive processing.

Lesson 005: A chip is built through a repeated fabrication loop of deposition, lithography, etch, doping, cleaning and inspection rather than in one printing step.

Lesson 006: Deposition adds ultra-thin films, and increasingly three-dimensional chip structures increase the value of precise deposition methods such as ALD.

Lesson 007: Etch removes selected material after lithography defines the pattern, and rising 3D complexity increases the value of high-precision plasma etch.

Lesson 008: Process control uses inspection, metrology and analytics to catch defects early, protect yield and prevent fabs from adding value to wafers that are already bad.

The big idea

A semiconductor process can be technologically impressive and still be economically terrible if too many chips come off the wafer defective.

That is why yield is one of the most important numbers in semiconductor manufacturing.

Yield = the percentage of manufactured dies that work well enough to sell.

A foundry is not paid for how many transistors it attempted to build. It is paid for usable silicon.

So the economics of a fab are roughly:

Wafer revenue opportunity → minus fabrication cost → divided across the good dies that survive.

If more dies survive, the effective manufacturing cost per good chip falls sharply.

1. Start with the wafer

A leading-edge fab usually processes 300mm wafers.

Many copies of the same chip design are patterned across one wafer. After fabrication, the wafer is cut into individual rectangles called dies.

Die = one individual piece of silicon that can become a chip.

Suppose a wafer physically contains 100 potential dies.

If 90 are usable, yield is 90%.

If only 50 are usable, yield is 50%.

The wafer still consumed roughly the same lithography, deposition, etch, clean, inspection, labor, utilities and factory time either way.

That is why yield has such powerful economic leverage.

2. A simple cost example

Assume one processed wafer costs $20,000.

Case A: 100 possible dies, 90 good dies.

Cost per good die = $20,000 / 90 = about $222.

Case B: 100 possible dies, 60 good dies.

Cost per good die = $20,000 / 60 = about $333.

Nothing about the nominal wafer cost changed.

But because fewer usable chips survived, the manufacturing cost per good die increased by roughly 50%.

This is why a small yield advantage can become a major gross-margin advantage.

3. Yield is not one number for the entire fab

There are several layers of yield.

First-pass intuition:

Process-step yield: did a specific manufacturing step work correctly?

Wafer yield: did the wafer make it through fabrication without being scrapped?

Die yield: how many individual dies on the wafer function correctly?

Final test yield: how many packaged chips pass electrical and reliability testing?

Packaging yield: did advanced packaging successfully connect the die, HBM, substrate and other components?

When people casually discuss semiconductor yield, they usually mean the share of dies or finished chips that become sellable products.

But economically, losses can happen at multiple stages.

4. Why larger chips are harder to yield

This is especially important for AI accelerators.

Imagine defects occur randomly across a wafer.

A tiny chip occupies a small area, so there is less surface area where a defect can land.

A very large chip occupies more area, so there are more opportunities for one defect to ruin it.

That means, all else equal:

Larger die → more defect exposure → lower expected die yield.

This is one reason giant AI accelerators are difficult and expensive to manufacture.

A high-end GPU may be physically much larger than a smartphone chip or small microcontroller.

If the process has a given defect density, the larger die suffers more.

5. Defect density

Defect density is a rough measure of how many yield-killing defects occur per unit of wafer area.

Lower defect density is better.

Suppose two fabs can both print the same transistor dimensions.

Fab A has fewer particles, fewer patterning errors, more stable etch, better overlay and tighter process control.

Fab B has more variation and more random defects.

They may advertise similar node names, but Fab A can have far better economics because more sellable chips emerge from each wafer.

This is a crucial point:

Node leadership is not only about transistor size or theoretical performance.

It is also about manufacturing repeatability at scale.

